Testing seon.schema-collection-order-test

FAIL in (no-stored-attribute-promises-an-order-the-database-cannot-keep) (schema_collection_order_test.clj:61)
no stored attribute is declared as an ordered collection
These stored attributes declare an ORDERED collection. A `:db.cardinality/many` attribute returns scalars sorted by VALUE and refs sorted by ENTITY ID, so insertion order is silently discarded — the shape round-trips, the order does not.

Fix each one:
  - order is NOT meaningful (almost always) -> `[:set X]`
  - order IS meaningful -> keep `[:set X]` and store the position explicitly on the child, the way `:seon.error/frames` renderers sort by frame ordinal.

Do NOT reach for `:db.type/tuple`: it caps at 8 values and kills element-level queries.

Offenders: [{:attr :portable.record/children, :form [:vector #:seon.db{:component true} [:map {:closed true} [:portable.record.child/value :portable.record.child/value] [:portable.record.child/render :portable.record.child/render]]]} {:attr :seon.agent.message/to, :form [:vector :seon.db/ref]} {:attr :seon.agent.run/forms, :form [:vector #:seon.db{:component true} :seon.db/ref]} {:attr :seon.agent.turn/evals, :form [:vector #:seon.db{:component true} :seon.db/ref]} {:attr :seon.agent.turn/timings, :form [:vector #:seon.db{:component true} :seon.db/ref]} {:attr :seon.render/children, :form [:vector #:seon.db{:component true} :seon.db/ref]}]
